WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.259
Film Courage:&nbsp; Michael, I’ve seen films
that have two nemesis working against the

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:06.589
hero but how about two heroes in a film?&nbsp;

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:07.819
Is that really done?

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:09.440
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; Yes.&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.090
That is done and it’s perfectly fine.&nbsp;

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.660
It’s primarily done in two kinds of movies.&nbsp;

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.349
Buddy movies and love stories or romantic
comedies.&nbsp;

00:00:21.349 --> 00:00:23.190
I guess that is three kinds?&nbsp;

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:28.599
But if you have two heroes, two protagonists,
all that means is that everything I’ve talked

00:00:28.599 --> 00:00:34.180
about in this interview about the hero, and
the hero’s six stages, and the hero’s

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.580
goal and the hero’s arc and so on, has to
apply to both.&nbsp;

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.980
So take a movie like PRETTY WOMAN.&nbsp;

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.640
You have two equal heroes, meaning they equally
are the main character, they both drive the

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.990
story forward, they are on the screen an equal
amount of time and so on.&nbsp;

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:53.510
And is typical in romantic comedies, each
of them has two goals.&nbsp;

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:56.650
His [Richard Gere’s character] is to put
together a deal for this shipping yard or

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:59.870
something, that is why he hires her [Julia
Roberts’ character] to pretend to be his

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:00.870
girlfriend.&nbsp;

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.809
And he starts to fall in love with her, so
his second goal is to win her love.&nbsp;

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:11.200
Her goal is to make three thousand bucks by
pretending to be his girlfriend and she gets

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.380
the three thousand dollars.&nbsp;

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.350
That is her finish line.&nbsp;

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:17.979
And she is falling in love with him, so her
other goal is to win his love.&nbsp;

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:22.299
And so now they are both pursuing a finish
line and in the end either they will both

00:01:22.299 --> 00:01:26.359
be together and achieve the goal or they won’t
be and they’ll fail.&nbsp;

00:01:26.359 --> 00:01:32.139
And then for each of them there must be those
five turning points and they must be able

00:01:32.139 --> 00:01:37.149
to divide them into those six stages and those
turning points will occur very close together.&nbsp;

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:42.170
Sometimes the turning points are identical
because when they first meet is the opportunity

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.289
for both of them.&nbsp;

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:47.170
Now when he hires her that is the change of
plans for both of them.&nbsp;

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.179
So that works just fine.&nbsp;

00:01:50.179 --> 00:01:54.799
Sometimes you will have one of those where
one of the heroes has an arc and the other

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:59.209
one might be fully evolved and be living in
their essence.

00:01:59.209 --> 00:02:06.619
But most of the time both characters are going
to have to grow and transform in parallel,

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:11.599
simultaneously if they go into the inner journey
for those characters.

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:13.540
Actually it is possible to have more than
two heroes.&nbsp;

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:19.840
If you take a movie like LOVE ACTUALLY, AMERICAN
GRAFFITI or anyone of those sort of ensemble

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:21.400
stories, CRASH and so on.&nbsp;

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.879
They have multiple heroes.&nbsp;

00:02:23.879 --> 00:02:29.590
A word to the wise: if you’re trying to
launch a career, until you are well-established,

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:35.300
I generally recommend against using multiple
hero stories, because no matter how good a

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:39.549
job you do, scripts with multiple heroes are
hard to read.&nbsp;

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:44.360
Because when we meet them on the screen no
one is going to confuse on the screen Sandra

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:49.110
Bullock with Don Cheadle or whoever else might
be in it.&nbsp;

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:53.970
But it is very easy to confuse the character
named Mary with a character named Susan and

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:58.780
we have to keep seven or eight heroes straight
just by their names on the page.&nbsp;

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:04.360
And it’s also very complex because you have
to carry seven different heroes through all

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:10.069
six stages and give them equal time and have
them all weave together.&nbsp;

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:13.829
That is usually something for later in your
career, at least after you’ve gotten some

00:03:13.829 --> 00:03:18.510
produced or had some scripts sold, unless
you pick a story and someone will pay you

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:19.510
to do it.&nbsp;

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.830
But it’s a tough kind of script to write
just because of the mechanics of it, let alone

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:26.689
because of the artistry involved in combining
it.&nbsp;

00:03:26.689 --> 00:03:30.930
But a two-hero story is very commercial and
is fine to pursue.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.439
Film Courage:&nbsp; Does the hero have to be human?&nbsp;

00:03:33.439 --> 00:03:34.780
Can it be a place?

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:35.780
Can it be an animal?&nbsp;

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.159
Barring animation, but can it be a place?

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:39.940
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; No.&nbsp;

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.430
It has to be a character.&nbsp;

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:52.830
It has to be an entity that takes action and
is pursuing some kind of goal.&nbsp;

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:55.650
Every character in a movie is going to have
some kind of goal they are going to want to

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:56.650
pursue.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:01.430
It may not take them to the climax like the
hero’s is, but it has to be an entity…I

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.249
don’t know how to say it better…and entity
that takes action.&nbsp;

00:04:05.249 --> 00:04:10.799
Certainly it can be in ZOOTOPIA as Judy Hopps
is the hero and she’s a bunny or a rabbit

00:04:10.799 --> 00:04:11.920
who is a cop.&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.810
So that’s fine.&nbsp;

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:17.109
It can be any kind of anthropomorphic character.&nbsp;

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:22.380
It can be a cyborg in I, Robot for instance
or A.I.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:28.150
Artificial Intelligence, the Steven Spielberg
movie or something like that.&nbsp;

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:29.710
But it has to be a character.&nbsp;

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:33.270
It can’t be a place, can’t be a quality,
can't be a collective noun.&nbsp;

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:38.220
The hero cannot be The Resistance.&nbsp;

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.710
The villain cannot be the Nazis.&nbsp;

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.759
These are terms that apply to individual characters.

00:04:43.759 --> 00:04:51.949
Question for the Viewers:&nbsp; What is your favorite
movie with two heroes?

00:04:51.949 --> 00:04:56.789
(Bonus clip) Film Courage:&nbsp; Take Stephen
King’s movie CHRISTINE.

00:04:56.789 --> 00:04:58.760
&nbsp; What about the car?

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:04.120
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; Well, the car wouldn’t
be the hero of that movie, it would be whoever

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.780
that kid was who was trying to stop Christine.&nbsp;

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:09.360
I don’t remember that movie…

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.750
Film Courage:&nbsp; Okay…[laughs] we can scratch
that.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:13.120
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; Well okay.&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.590
If you don’t want to scratch it, [the car]
Christine would be an anthropomorphic character

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:21.210
because it takes action.&nbsp;

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.460
It rolls and talks or whatever it says.&nbsp;

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.630
You could consider it a character, yes.

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:28.099
Film Courage:&nbsp; Or even K.I.T.T. in Knight
Rider?&nbsp;

00:05:28.099 --> 00:05:31.750
I know we are talking about television here…was
a car.

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:37.669
Michael Huge:&nbsp; We’re talking about television….we’re
talking about bad television!&nbsp;

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:39.479
Bad cinema.&nbsp;

00:05:39.479 --> 00:05:41.930
This is Film Courage, not Film Crap!&nbsp;

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:45.600
What are you…what are you doing to your
show here?

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.420
Film Courage:&nbsp; The thing is, we are showing
an example, though of how something doesn’t

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:50.789
have to be human.&nbsp;

00:05:50.789 --> 00:05:53.080
It can be a machine, it can be anthropomorphic.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.900
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; And what could go wrong?&nbsp;

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:02.599
Because we’ve come up with so many good
examples of machine heroes.&nbsp;

00:06:02.599 --> 00:06:04.540
Film Courage:&nbsp; [Laughs]&nbsp; How about R2-D2?

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.470
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; I know you’re going to
cut this out [laughs].&nbsp;

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.160
But I really want you to keep this segment
of the video in.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:10.160
Film Courage:&nbsp; This is for the bonus features.

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.650
Michael Hauge:&nbsp; This is an important lesson
for everyone.&nbsp;

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.819
Watch out for the machine heroes, you know!

00:06:14.819 --> 00:06:16.219
Film Courage:&nbsp; Okay…[laughing] we can stop.

